    "text": "To determine the length of the belt, we need to analyze the configuration of the belt around the three circles. Each circle has a radius of 10 cm, and the belt is drawn tightly around them, forming a triangular configuration with the centers of the circles.\n\n### Step 1: Determine the side lengths of the triangle\nThe centers of the three circles form an equilateral triangle with each side equal to the diameter of one circle. The diameter of each circle is \\(2 \\times 10 = 20\\) cm. Therefore, the side length of the equilateral triangle is 20 cm.\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the perimeter of the equilateral triangle\nThe perimeter \\(P\\) of the equilateral triangle is:\n\\[\nP = 3 \\times 20 = 60 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the length of the belt along the arcs\nThe belt also wraps around the arcs where the circles touch. Each arc is a quarter-circle because the belt touches the circles at the points where the arcs meet. The radius of each quarter-circle is 10 cm.\n\nThe length of one quarter-circle arc is:\n\\[\n\\text{Arc length} = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 2\\pi \\times 10 = 5\\pi \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nSince there are three such quarter-circles, the total length of the arcs is:\n\\[\n3 \\times 5\\pi = 15\\pi \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Calculate the total length of the belt\nThe total length of the belt is the sum of the perimeter of the equilateral triangle and the total length of the arcs:\n\\[\n\\text{Total length} = 60 + 15\\pi \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\n### Step 5: Identify \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)\nFrom the expression \\(60 + 15\\pi\\), we identify:\n\\[\na = 60 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 15\n\\]\n\n### Step 6: Calculate \\(a + b\\)\n\\[\na + b = 60 + 15 = 75\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a + b\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{75}\n\\]",
    "timing_scope": "native prompt render, tokenize, transfer, CUDA-synchronized generate, decode; excludes loading/scoring",
